Seven models that describe an agent, so that an agent is rows rather than Python. `AIAgent` carries the name, the description, the system prompt, the model, and the generation overrides. `AIAgentTool` binds a tool to an agent, `AIAgentSubagent` binds a specialist to a supervisor, and `AIAgentSkill` binds a block of rules an agent loads on demand. `AITool` catalogs a tool that did not come from an MCP server, and `AISkill` holds the rules. `AIAgentThread` records one LangGraph conversation and gives its checkpoints a handle.

`AITool` is deliberately the same shape as `MCPTool`. An approval gate has to ask three questions of every tool - is this offered, does it need a person, and has its definition moved - and a second tool source that could not answer them would walk past the gate the first one cannot.

Three tool sources: an MCP tool, a Python callable a consuming app registered with `@register_ai_tool`, and a Nautobot Job. A Job tool starts its Job and reports the Job Result immediately. It does not wait, because an agent loop is bounded and a queue can outlast it.

A new **Agents** group on the shared **AI Tools** tab, at weight 300. A sibling app that wants its own group now takes 400 or higher; `docs/dev/extending.md` says so and its table is corrected.

A Git datasource for Python tools. A repository that provides **AI tools** carries an `ai_tools` module; Nautobot clones it, the app imports it, and every `@register_ai_tool` in it becomes an `AITool` record of kind `git` pointing back at the repository. Same directory convention, same private import and same trust boundary as a Git-sourced Job.

The registry is per process, so the agent builder imports a repository on the first miss rather than relying on the process that ran the sync. A record names a repository and the fixed `ai_tools` module inside it; it never names an import path.
